<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bible</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bible is an anthology of religious texts central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and important in other Abrahamic religions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, compiled by different religious communities into official collections called biblical canons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> with some parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, is divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, Nevi'im and Ketuvim; its oldest parts may be as early as c. 1200 BCE. The Christian Bible joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>, a reorganisation of the Hebrew Bible's books and the deuterocanonical books, to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> written mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> in the 1st century CE. With estimated sales of over five billion copies, it is the best-selling publication of all time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bible.jpg" alt="Bible" width="330" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NYC Wanderer (Kevin Eng) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gutenberg_Bible,_Lenox_Copy,_New_York_Public_Library,_2009._Pic_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greeks called papyrus byblos after the city through which they obtained it, and from that word came biblion, book, and ultimately the word <b>Bible</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zürich Bible, printed by Christoph Froschauer, was completed in 1531, three years before Martin Luther’s complete German <b>Bible</b> appeared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>These are the kings known from sources outside the <b>Bible</b>; the last unambiguous reference to Edom is an Assyrian inscription of 667 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Stability came only with Omri, an army commander who seized power c. 885 BCE and founded a new capital on the hill of Samaria, which he bought, according to the <b>Bible</b>, from a man named Shemer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A few years after the division, around 925 BCE, the pharaoh Shoshenq I, the biblical Shishak, campaigned in the region; the <b>Bible</b> says Rehoboam bought him off with the treasures of the temple and palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Codex Amiatinus, made at Wearmouth-Jarrow and the oldest surviving complete Latin <b>Bible</b>, was long thought to be Italian until its Northumbrian origin was identified in the 1880s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
